  <!-- ═══ 5. A DIFFERENT WAY OF UNDERSTANDING — bg-header ══════════════════ -->
  <section class="bg-header text-header-foreground">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-20 md:py-28 max-w-5xl">

      <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-5xl text-gold-gradient leading-tight">
          A Different Way Of Understanding The Problem
        </h2>
        <span class="h2-accent"></span>

        <div class="space-y-4 font-body text-sm md:text-base text-header-foreground/55 leading-relaxed">
          <p>For most of human history, the transition into adulthood did not happen in isolation. Young men were gradually shaped through responsibility, challenge, guidance, brotherhood, mentorship, embodied practice, meaningful expectations, and relationships with older men capable of helping them mature psychologically and emotionally.</p>
          <p>Today, many young men are attempting to navigate that transition largely alone. They are surrounded by stimulation, information, pressure, comparison, distraction, and performance demands, yet often lack meaningful spaces where they can slow down enough to develop genuine self-understanding, emotional steadiness, direction, and internal structure.</p>
          <p>The structures that once made this kind of developmental support available naturally — extended family networks, close neighbourhood relationships, religious communities, mentorship from trusted non-parental adults, structured transitions into responsibility — have largely dissolved in modern life. "It takes a village" was never merely a sentiment. It was a structural reality of human development, maintained in various forms across virtually every culture for thousands of years.</p>
          <p>That village is largely gone. What has replaced it is, primarily, the nuclear family: one or two parents carrying a developmental load that was never designed to be carried by one or two people alone. Parents are asked to be everything — provider and mentor, authority figure and emotional safe haven, boundary-setter and friend. It is a genuinely impossible position. And the young man, meanwhile, is largely left to figure out adulthood through his own efforts, peer influence, and the often contradictory signals of popular culture and social media.</p>
          <p class="font-medium text-header-foreground/70">This is not a failure of parenting. It is a structural absence.</p>
          <p>The role of someone outside the family — a person without the personal history, unspoken expectations, and emotional entanglement that parents naturally and unavoidably carry, yet still genuinely invested in the young man's flourishing — is an ancient and real one. Not a therapist. Not a teacher. A developmental mentor: someone capable of honest reflection, consistent accountability, and real relationship without the weight of personal investment distorting every exchange. This work is a contemporary version of that role.</p>
          <p>In many cases, the issue is not simply motivation. It is disconnection: from self, from responsibility, from embodied presence, from meaningful challenge, and from trustworthy forms of guidance.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Pull quote 2 -->
      <blockquote class="pl-6 border-l-2 border-primary/40 mb-12 max-w-2xl">
        <p class="font-heading text-2xl md:text-3xl text-header-foreground/85 leading-snug mb-2">
          The question is not always "what is wrong with him?"
        </p>
        <p class="font-heading text-xl md:text-2xl text-primary/60 leading-snug">
          Sometimes the better question is: "What has he not yet been given?"
        </p>
      </blockquote>

      <!-- Contrast panel -->
      <div class="border border-header-foreground/10 rounded-2xl p-8 md:p-10 bg-void/20 mb-12">
        <h3 class="font-heading text-xs text-gold-gradient mb-8 tracking-widest uppercase">
          What This Work Is, and What It Is Not
        </h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 md:gap-8">
          <div>
            <p class="font-heading text-sm text-header-foreground/75 mb-3">Not Performance Coaching</p>
            <p class="font-body text-xs text-header-foreground/45 leading-relaxed">
              This work is not focused on turning young men into hyper-productive achievement machines or forcing them into externally imposed identities. The goal is not performance at any cost, but the development of grounded self-awareness, responsibility, resilience, and inner stability.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-heading text-sm text-header-foreground/75 mb-3">Not Passive Emotional Processing</p>
            <p class="font-body text-xs text-header-foreground/45 leading-relaxed">
              While emotional reflection is important, this work is not based on endless analysis without movement or accountability. Insight must gradually become embodied through action, responsibility, communication, self-observation, and lived change.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-heading text-sm text-header-foreground/75 mb-3">Not Spiritual Escapism</p>
            <p class="font-body text-xs text-header-foreground/45 leading-relaxed">
              Meditation and breathwork are approached practically as tools for attention, nervous system regulation, emotional awareness, and self-observation. This work is grounded in real life: relationships, study, work, discipline, health, responsibility, and honest engagement with reality.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-4 font-body text-sm md:text-base text-header-foreground/55 leading-relaxed mb-10">
        <p>Many young men do not need to become somebody else entirely. They need support in becoming more deeply connected to themselves: more honest, more grounded, more capable of responsibility, more emotionally mature, and more able to consciously shape the direction of their lives.</p>
        <p>That process happens slowly through relationship, reflection, challenge, practice, and trust built over time.</p>
      </div>

      <a href="#how-it-works" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-primary/55 hover:text-primary text-sm font-body transition-colors">
        Explore how the mentorship works
        <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M5 12h14M12 5l7 7-7 7" />
        </svg>
      </a>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══ 6. WHAT THIS WORK DEVELOPS — bg-background — scroll carousel ═════ -->
